  The covariance matrix looks like this: 
\[
\begin{bmatrix}
1 & 2 \\
2 & 3
\end{bmatrix}
\begin{bmatrix}
3 & 4 \\
1 & 5
\end{bmatrix}
= \begin{bmatrix}
1\cdot 3 + 2\cdot 1 & 1\cdot 4 + 2\cdot 5 \\
2\cdot 3 + 3\cdot 1 & 2\cdot 4 + 3\cdot 5
\end{bmatrix}
=\begin{bmatrix}
5 & 14 \\
11 & 23
\end{bmatrix}
\]
A key property of eigenvalues we will use is the fact that the sum of the eigenvalues of a matrix equals the trace of the matrix.  The trace of a matrix is the sum of the elements on the main diagonal. The trace of our matrix is $5+23 = 28$, so the sum of the eigenvalues is 28. The answer is (J).
